            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that this houseis fairly leaky and has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** The old bathroom exhaust fan



** At connections between walls, ceilings, and floors, particularly in the master bedroom



** It appears that in the exterior corner of the master bedroom, there is a spot where the insulation is either missing or damaged, which would warrent further investigation 



** Around the header space between the floor joists in the basement, and around the top of the wood storage section of the basement



** Weatherstripping around exterior doors should be checked regularly, and replaced as necessary. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $220 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.4 GJ. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of this house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation, with an additional 2 inches of EPS board insulation (R10) installed as part of a previous siding upgrade. I recommend you add insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding, to bring the exterior walls up to a minimum of R30. In my computer model, I have done this by replacing the 2 inches of EPS insulation with 4 inches (R20) of XPS board insulation, although there are different options available for this kind of upgrade. 



Savings: By adding a minimum R10 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $340 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.7 GJ. </mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R28 of insulation, comprised of 7.5 inches of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R60 using blown cellulose insulation. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R28 up to a minimum R60, this is estimated to save $190 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.8 GJ.



</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with R7.5 rigid board insulation. I have modeled replacing the current insulation with 2x6 framing at 16 inches off centre, and insulating the framing with R20 batt insulation. This would double the R value of the insulation on the walls. 



As with the main walls, there are many options for how to go about adding insulation to the basement of this house. I recommend that you consider what you are planning on using the space for prior to deciding on what to do with the insulation in the basement. Currently it is being used for storage and a utility room, which is why I have given this upgrade a low priority despite the high savings. 



Savings: By replacing the R7.5 rigid insulation currently on the basement walls with 2x6 framing with 5.5 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, you could potentially save $1,250 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 31.4 GJ.</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house are older double pane windows. They are generally in good shape, but could be replaced should you be doing an upgrade to the exterior insulation. 



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, Arctic Energy Alliance recommends full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Your doors are both steel doors with polystyrene insulation. Should you replace these doors, we recommend ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $230  per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.9 GJ. This does not include any potential savings from replacing the doors. </windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 liter oil fired hot water tank. The efficiency factor of this tank is assumed to be 0.53. Should this domestic hot water tank need to be replaced, we recommend looking for a tank with a minimum energy factor of 0.6. 



Savings: By replacing your current oil fired hot water heater with an oil fired tank water heater with an energy factor of 0.60, you could potentially save $60 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by1.6 GJ.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified direct vent, non condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with an 87% AFUE direct vent, non condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $900 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 20.4 GJ. 



Our current advice recommends a a 95% efficient, direct vent, condensing oil furnace, which will result in additional savings above what is listed here. However, I selected this type of furnace based on what is likely to be available in the community of Tulita. I recommend that you reach out to see what is available before making your final selection. 

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Thermal Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal, and LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



I recommend that all lighting in this home be replaced with LED lighting, including both single bulbs and the fluorescent tubes in the basement.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 9.8 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil furnace and basement wood stove are both susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of this home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



This home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood   

        - one bathroom fan in the master bedroom ensuite

        - a clothes dryer



I recommend installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Tulita



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Tulita:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil - $1.51 per liter 

Wood - $735 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
